<?xml version="1.0"?>
<rdf:RDF xmlns="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo#"
     xml:base="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo"
     xmlns:heo="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo#"
     xmlns:owl="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#"
     xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#"
     xmlns:xml="http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace"
     xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#"
     xmlns:foaf="http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/"
     xmlns:hewc="https://healtheconomics.wiki/concept/"
     xmlns:rdfs="http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#"
     xmlns:skos="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#"
     xmlns:vann="http://purl.org/vocab/vann/"
     xmlns:dcterms="http://purl.org/dc/terms/">
    <owl:Ontology rdf:about="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo">
        <owl:versionIRI rdf:resource="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo/2.0.0"/>
        <dcterms:created rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-09-30</dcterms:created>
        <dcterms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0000-0001-9502-4678"/>
        <dcterms:creator rdf:resource="https://orcid.org/0009-0006-0231-3731"/>
        <dcterms:description xml:lang="en">A vocabulary of health economics concepts, with definitions and typed links between them, taken from HealthEconomics.wiki. Each concept is identified by a stable URI that serves as its permanent identifier. Concepts are SKOS concepts arranged by skos:broader under one top concept, Health Economics, and the wiki&apos;s subdomains; a concept with no is-type-of parent is placed in that subject structure with heo:classifiedUnder. Six relationships (part of, measures, used to calculate, treats, risk factor for, precedes) are OWL object properties with named inverses, and the others are descriptive annotation properties. HEO identifies concepts; its companion RHEO defines types classified within the Basic Formal Ontology. A concept and a type never share an identifier.</dcterms:description>
        <dcterms:license rdf:resource="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"/>
        <dcterms:modified rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#date">2026-11-08</dcterms:modified>
        <dcterms:publisher>Intelligent Parameters Ltd</dcterms:publisher>
        <dcterms:title xml:lang="en">Health Economics Ontology (HEO)</dcterms:title>
        <vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>heo</vann:preferredNamespacePrefix>
        <vann:preferredNamespaceUri>https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo#</vann:preferredNamespaceUri>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Address policy: concepts live at https://healtheconomics.wiki/concept/&lt;slug&gt; (prefix hewc:); HEO&apos;s own terms at https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo# (prefix heo:). Each prefix stands for one address only.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every link between two concepts has a permanent identifier (HEO-L-...), recorded as an OWL axiom annotation (dcterms:identifier); the ID is made from the two concepts and the link type, so it stays the same across versions. Concepts also record the learning pathway framework steps they sit on (heo:coreConceptIn, heo:optionalConceptIn).</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Release 1.3.0 holds 4009 concepts in use and 1192 deprecated concepts kept so their URIs never break. Review status is recorded on each concept (heo:reviewStatus). In this release 1280 concepts with a full article are expert verified and 2729 dictionary entries are not yet expert verified. Inclusion in HEO does not mean that a concept has been expert verified.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Version 2.0.0 moves every HEO address from https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontology/heo to https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo, so the ontology identifier, its version identifier and HEO's own terms (prefix heo:) match the ontology page on the site. Concept addresses (https://healtheconomics.wiki/concept/&lt;slug&gt;) do not change. Every concept, definition and relationship is the same as in 1.3.1. The old /ontology/ addresses are retired and are not redirected.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Version 1.3.1 changes the file layout only. Every concept, definition and relationship is the same as in 1.3.0. The OWL file is now written in the standard flat layout, with the ontology header, property declarations and concepts at the top level.</rdfs:comment>
        <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://healtheconomics.wiki/"/>
        <owl:priorVersion rdf:resource="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontology/heo/1.3.1"/>
        <owl:versionInfo>2.0.0</owl:versionInfo>
        <skos:editorialNote xml:lang="en">Reading convention (1.1.0): every link between two HEO concepts is a conceptual or methodological association between meanings, never an unrestricted factual claim about particular people, organisations or events. Links such as treats, risk factor for and performs carry a scope note saying how to read them and point to the RHEO relations for real-world statements. Application contract: each HEO link type has heo:applicationUse values (navigation, learning, formal inference). skos:broader is the navigation tree and holds two kinds of link, is-type-of links (heo:isTypeOf, also used for formal inference) and placement links (heo:classifiedUnder, navigation only); skos:related is used only for navigation. Learning activities use only links whose heo:linkReviewStatus is machine-reviewed or curator-approved; navigation may show unreviewed links but marks them as unreviewed. A link whose subject is a named organisation, law or instrument (for example NICE or FDA) is a dated factual claim: it needs a source and a review date, and a link true only in the past is marked historical.</skos:editorialNote>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Health Economics Ontology (HEO)</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">HEO concepts are units of meaning in health economics: what a term means and how it relates to other terms. They are not the real-world things those terms are about. Patients, care episodes, resources and other real-world entities and processes are described in the companion reality ontology RHEO. Links between HEO concepts state conceptual or methodological relations, not claims about events in the world or about the effects of treatments. Policy (Darrin Baines, 15 Oct 2026): HEO identifies concepts; RHEO defines types classified within the Basic Formal Ontology. A concept and its associated RHEO type have different identities, even when their names are similar: an HEO concept URI is never used as a RHEO type identifier, and each connection between them is stated explicitly in the RHEO to HEO mapping file.</skos:scopeNote>
        <foaf:homepage rdf:resource="https://healtheconomics.wiki/ontologies/heo"/>
    </owl:Ontology>
    


